WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.840
Quick announcement up top—I’m now&nbsp;
partnered with Dragon Shield! Follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.280
my affiliate link in the description to get&nbsp;
a discount on your online sleeve purchases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:10.960
and keep an eye out in the coming weeks&nbsp;
for an awesome custom sleeve design that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:15.600
me and my artist friend are cooking&nbsp;
up at the moment. Now, onto the video.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.440
Lorwyn Eclipsed is here, and my patrons&nbsp;
have decreed that I will be reviewing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.400
-1/-1 counter precon. Which is great, because&nbsp;
Blight Curse has been a lot of fun to dig into,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.360
and is pretty phenomenal as far as preconstructed&nbsp;
commander decks go in my opinion. Not because it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.240
a perfect deck, and we can work on that part&nbsp;
in a bit, but for a lot of other reasons we’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:38.720
be getting into. As usual, I’m going to start by&nbsp;
talking about what this deck is trying to do, then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:43.120
I’ll be discussing what I like about it, and I’ll&nbsp;
finish up with some ways it could be improved.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.600
To start with, let’s talk about this deck’s&nbsp;
main commander, Auntie Ool. She is a 4 mana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:52.320
4/4 with Ward: Blight 2, and “Whenever one&nbsp;
or more -1 counters are put on a creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:57.280
draw a card if you control that creature. If you&nbsp;
don’t control it, its controller loses 1 life.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:02.720
The first thing I notice about this is a lack of&nbsp;
limiting phrases in this text box—no “one or more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:08.160
creatures”, and no once per turn. What this means&nbsp;
is that this has the potential to be a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.680
explosive value generator, easily drawing 5 or&nbsp;
more cards per turn with the right setup present.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.720
On a 4 mana 4/4 commander with a protective&nbsp;
ability on it, that’s pretty good. The downside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:24.960
here is the specificity—in total there are an okay&nbsp;
number of cards that put -1 counters on creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.800
but it’s not a particularly generous pool to&nbsp;
work with when you consider that these cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:34.160
function in a wide variety of different ways. Some&nbsp;
put -1 counters on all creatures, rewarding wide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:38.960
boards with massive card advantage, whereas others&nbsp;
gain -1 counters over time, rewarding a slower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:44.080
grindier playstyle. Plenty of others are one time&nbsp;
use, and a lot of cards that use -1 counters are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:49.040
primarily intended to put counters on opposing&nbsp;
creatures and are balanced with this in mind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:53.920
Do you place these on your own creatures instead&nbsp;
for a bit of card advantage? All this to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:56.960
Auntie Ool is a powerful commander&nbsp;
within her own synergy sphere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.840
and the challenge in building her&nbsp;
is going to be navigating a limited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:04.720
card pool to create a deck that plays to her&nbsp;
strengths without ending up too disjointed.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:08.880
Looking at this deck’s cards that place -1&nbsp;
counters, there are 9 cards that specifically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.760
place -1 counters on your creatures, another&nbsp;
9 that place -1 counters on any creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.920
2 cards that place counters on each opposing&nbsp;
creature, and 4 cards that place counters on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:23.760
all creatures. This array of different -1 counter&nbsp;
cards is accompanied by a number of payoff cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.680
which likewise operate in a variety of&nbsp;
different ways. 8 of these trigger when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:31.520
creatures with -1 counters on them die,&nbsp;
with most of these either providing card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:36.720
advantage or reanimating the creatures under your&nbsp;
control. Another 4 cards plus the commander reward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.040
the act of placing counters itself, and the&nbsp;
synergies get much more granular from here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.800
including amplifier cards like Evolution&nbsp;
Sage, cards that modify the effect of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:50.080
-1 counters like Kulrath Knight, and weird&nbsp;
roundabout synergies like Tree of Perdition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:54.000
Some of the setup and payoff combinations here&nbsp;
are quite good, especially the ones involving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.880
cards that place -1 counters onto all creatures,&nbsp;
and I expect that piecing together one of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:03.200
zingy pairings is a big way this deck will try&nbsp;
to get ahead. Naturally, this deck has a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.120
of tools to slow opposing creature-based&nbsp;
gameplans given its focus on -1 counters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.520
and these creature-shrinking options are joined&nbsp;
by 5 cards that deal with artifacts, enchantments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:16.560
or both. Rounding out the 99 of this deck&nbsp;
are moderate counts of both draw and ramp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:21.200
much of which involves -1/-1 counters in some way.&nbsp;
Through some powerful synergies, I expect this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:26.080
deck will try to get ahead in explosive fashion,&nbsp;
slow opposing gameplans with interaction, and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.560
gradually wear down opponents with a mixture&nbsp;
of creature attacks and incremental damage.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.640
So, to start with what I like about this deck,&nbsp;
this strikes me as quite a “Johnny” sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:39.040
precon. Because the deck was drawing from a&nbsp;
limited card pool, the -1 counter synergies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:43.040
take a variety of different shapes, and the&nbsp;
setups and payoffs within this deck piece&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:48.640
together in some really cool, and sometimes really&nbsp;
powerful, ways. Just thinking about Auntie Ool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:53.520
the cards that place -1 counters on all creatures&nbsp;
end up being a Shamanic Revelation for you and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:57.920
Rakdos Charm for your opponents, both effects&nbsp;
that can be very impactful depending on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:02.400
state of the game. Any proliferate effects&nbsp;
can amplify the effect of previous counters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:06.720
creating further value and damage at a brisk&nbsp;
pace. These plays can also be doubled down on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:11.440
with an additional payoff card like Hapatra or&nbsp;
Flourishing Defenses, which can put you in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:16.240
driving seat of a game fast. The deck also has&nbsp;
two different cards that reanimate creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:21.040
that die with -1 counters on them, both at three&nbsp;
mana, and this means that it’s possible to perform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:25.680
some powerful creature-stealing maneuvers&nbsp;
pretty early in the game. All this to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:29.600
this deck can do some powerful things, and the&nbsp;
way it operates seems like it’d make for an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:34.560
exciting play experience for any players who are&nbsp;
especially interested in the way cards synergize.

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:38.160
Just a minute ago I talked about the two cards&nbsp;
that reanimate opposing creatures that die with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:42.640
-1/-1 counters on them, and one of these two is&nbsp;
actually the alternate commander for this deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:47.040
The Reaper King No More. And, that’s&nbsp;
another thing I like about this deck:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:51.760
it actually feels like it has an alternate&nbsp;
commander. The power level of the card is nice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:55.760
being basically a sidegrade of Necroskitter that&nbsp;
trades some raw power for a bit more utility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:00.400
and the deck does have a fair amount of cards that&nbsp;
place -1 counters on opposing creatures in it. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:04.240
this deck has a better relationship with its&nbsp;
alt commander than most precons in the sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:08.240
that if you really like this card, you can&nbsp;
run it as the commander and probably have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:12.160
an okay time with it. If you were doing that&nbsp;
long-term you’d want to tweak the deck a bit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:17.040
since Auntie Ool is pretty pivotal to the deck’s&nbsp;
card economy, but The Reaper is already much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:20.480
closer to feeling good as a commander in&nbsp;
this deck than most alternate commanders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:24.400
and I’ll talk a bit later about some&nbsp;
possible ways to adapt this deck to him.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:28.800
As usual, I playtested the deck 20 times and&nbsp;
kept track of how the first 5 turns went in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:33.280
an excel doc. Overall, it felt decent—there&nbsp;
weren’t many games where the deck was really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:37.520
popping off super early like I had when I was&nbsp;
testing the Worldshaper precon last year, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:42.240
most games the deck was doing some decent stuff&nbsp;
and setting up for cool plays later in the game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.880
One cool thing is how varied the deck felt—there&nbsp;
are so many different ways the different synergy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:51.120
elements can be pieced together, whether that be&nbsp;
making a value engine, making a removal engine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:56.160
having a single explosive turn, putting together&nbsp;
a wide board, and so on. The commander also really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:00.480
helps with making the deck feel good, for two&nbsp;
reasons. For one, she offers an easy default&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:04.880
-1 counter payoff, reducing the strain on&nbsp;
the deck to find other payoffs. Secondly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:09.680
the fact that she’s a powerful draw engine allows&nbsp;
somebody piloting the deck to see more of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:13.600
which is super useful for a deck whose cards&nbsp;
are highly varied in how they work and what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:18.000
they accomplish. As a final note about what I&nbsp;
like here, the manabase is fine enough for what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:22.480
this deck is doing. Upgrade it if you like,&nbsp;
but I’ll be leaving it alone in my tweaks.

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:27.440
Getting into what I dislike about playing this&nbsp;
deck, the big thing is dead cards. I had several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:31.600
playtests where I kept accumulating cards in&nbsp;
hand that were entirely useless in my current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:35.280
gamestate. A lot of these are cards that are&nbsp;
just a bit more specific in their play pattern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:39.200
or in the way they synergize with other cards,&nbsp;
being a little niche or hard to fully utilize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:43.520
with the current tools. There is one frequently&nbsp;
dead card we should get out of the way first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:49.040
though—Liliana, Death Wielder is an intro deck&nbsp;
planeswalker from Amonkhet, and it is truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:54.800
godawful. It’d be barely playable at 4 mana, and&nbsp;
in reality it’s 7 mana, because it’s an intro deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:59.600
planeswalker. I guess this was added to this deck&nbsp;
to be an educational first cut for new players,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:04.320
a la Temple of the False God, but I don’t like&nbsp;
that an iconic planeswalker like Liliana is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:08.640
reduced to serving that role here. Anyway,&nbsp;
that’s our first cut, and it should be a cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:12.720
basically regardless of what you want to do with&nbsp;
this deck. Another thing I dislike about this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:18.080
deck’s current form is its creature count—this&nbsp;
feels like a deck that wants to be running 35-40&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:23.360
creatures based on the number of cards that care&nbsp;
about creatures, but instead it’s running 27.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:27.360
This adds to the awkwardness, when the deck&nbsp;
keeps drawing into enchantments and sorceries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:31.600
and struggles to get much use out of something&nbsp;
like Soul Snuffers or Grave Venerations that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:36.160
really wants more creatures floating around in&nbsp;
the ecosystem. So, these are the main things I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:41.200
want to be focused on in my upgrade: reducing&nbsp;
dead draws, and upping the creature count.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:45.920
Before I get into my other 9 cuts, though, I want&nbsp;
to talk about alternative build paths, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:50.160
that’s another reason I really like this precon&nbsp;
as a product. There are a lot of little elements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:55.120
here that could easily spark some inspiration and&nbsp;
be expanded outward. A few proliferate cards help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:59.200
the deck amplify its -1 counters, and one way&nbsp;
to modify this deck could be to lean harder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:03.440
into that mechanic and add other cards that&nbsp;
can utilize it such as Astral Cornucopia and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:08.400
Twitching Doll. There may not be a massive pool&nbsp;
of -1 counter cards in the game, but there sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:12.880
are a lot of other counter-using cards around that&nbsp;
can utilize some of the same payoffs and support.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:17.840
Another synergy area this deck gives a taste of is&nbsp;
graveyard synergies—there are several cards that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:22.480
use graveyards, as well as Archfiend of Ifnir that&nbsp;
cares about discarding. These aren’t very well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:26.480
supported now, but you could introduce some more&nbsp;
looting effects into the deck to help with these,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:30.640
and this extra filtering would also likely help&nbsp;
the deck in general in the pursuit of assembling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:34.640
engines. Finally, you could also adapt the&nbsp;
deck to work better with The Reaper in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:38.560
command zone. There are several great cards that&nbsp;
put -1 counters on opposing creatures that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:43.280
could add if you don’t mind paying a bit, such as&nbsp;
Persistent Constrictor and Spitting Dilophosaurus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:47.680
and these paired with a bit more draw would likely&nbsp;
make for a very different, but still functional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:51.280
playstyle. This would also likely work&nbsp;
well paired with a more graveyard-heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:55.280
or proliferate-heavy approach as well—you can&nbsp;
do a bit of mixing and matching among these.

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:58.720
For my upgrade, I’m choosing to go for a&nbsp;
more streamlined approach that eliminates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:02.400
some potential deadweight and shores up some weak&nbsp;
spots, while increasing the creature count and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:07.680
improving the tools for incremental -1 counters.&nbsp;
Let’s start with cuts. Archfiend of Ifnir is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:12.880
getting cut because this deck has 5 other cards&nbsp;
that discard, and 3 of those are cycling lands.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:16.480
This was one of my first observations after&nbsp;
plugging this deck into Archidekt—I saw an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:20.800
Archfiend of Ifnir, I dropped “fo:discard”&nbsp;
into the search box, and boom, that’s an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:24.880
easy cut unless you’re feeling highly motivated&nbsp;
to get more self-discard into this bad boy. I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:28.640
also cutting Puca’s Covenant and Persist due&nbsp;
to this deck’s lack of graveyard-enabling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:32.720
The former requires your creatures dying and&nbsp;
stuff in your graveyard to be gotten back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:36.560
which is too niche a set of requirements for&nbsp;
the current form of this deck. Persist will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:41.520
be useful a bit more often, but its ceiling is&nbsp;
also much lower, so it doesn’t excite me either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:45.120
I am keeping the rest of the graveyard synergies&nbsp;
around, though, as they have a lot more utility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:50.480
than these two. Speaking of persist, Puppeteer&nbsp;
Clique—this card looks like it synergizes with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:54.560
the deck, but in practice you’ll probably be&nbsp;
struggling not to turn off its keyword more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:59.840
often than you’ll be enjoying that extra Auntie&nbsp;
Ool trigger. I’m also cutting Grim Poppet—this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:04.560
one is a bit painful as it’s a funky card with&nbsp;
some neat possibilities here, but 7 is such a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:08.560
large amount of mana to pay for something&nbsp;
with no guarantee of doing much. From here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:13.040
the final four cuts are noncreature filler, which&nbsp;
will be getting replaced with creatures and other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:17.360
options better suited to this deck. Harmonize&nbsp;
and Hoarder’s Greed are getting cut for alternate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:21.840
draw, Commander’s Sphere for an alternate mana&nbsp;
rock, and Cathartic Pyre for a better removal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:26.800
spell. I will say, the fact that I am spending 4&nbsp;
of my 10 swaps targeting perfectly passable filler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:32.000
speaks well to this deck’s card quality—there’s&nbsp;
just not many truly objectionable cards here.

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:36.000
Now for the additions. I’m starting here&nbsp;
with four low cost creatures with blight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:40.880
abilities from the Lorwyn: Eclipsed main&nbsp;
set: Dawnhand Dissident, Gristle Glutton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:46.000
Scuzzback Scrounger, and Shadow Urchin. Adding&nbsp;
these lowers our curve, ups our creature count,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:50.320
makes it easier to trigger the commander&nbsp;
on-demand, and as an added bonus 3 of the 4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:54.560
have fun, alliterative names. You could also add&nbsp;
the haste one if you wanted to, but I don’t see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:59.280
this deck wanting haste on a single creature all&nbsp;
that often. As replacement creature-based draw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:03.680
I’m adding Generous Patron, which can potentially&nbsp;
net quite a solid amount of cards. To replace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:08.160
the mana rock and creature removal we cut, I’m&nbsp;
adding Staff of Compleation and Grim Affliction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:13.040
both of which can also proliferate. From here, I’m&nbsp;
also a bit concerned about this deck’s ability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:17.840
maintain a healthy life total—it’s on the grindier&nbsp;
side, there was already some big lifepay in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:21.920
deck, and we just added some more big life&nbsp;
pay in the form of Staff of Completion. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:26.960
the remaining additions will also perform a&nbsp;
stabilizing role here. Obelisk Spider is great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:30.560
as a source of incremental damage, pairing&nbsp;
with the commander to pepper down opponents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:35.600
at a steady pace and gaining a bit of life in the&nbsp;
process. Pest Infestation boosts the deck’s count&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:39.440
of artifact and enchantment destruction,&nbsp;
while also gaining life and cementing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:45.120
advantages this deck gains from having a wide&nbsp;
board. Finally, Elegy Acolyte gives a nice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:50.080
lifelinking body in addition to some draw, further&nbsp;
smoothing this deck out in multiple regards. —

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:53.520
So this is our finished version of the deck, with&nbsp;
significantly expanded connective tissue and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:59.680
creature count of 32, up from 27. I will say that&nbsp;
in my tweaks here, I was very conservative with my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:05.440
cuts to cards that pressure opposing life totals.&nbsp;
I don’t know that Grave Titan, Lasting Tarfire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:09.600
Tree of Perdition, or Flourishing Defenses&nbsp;
belong here long term, but this is definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:13.600
one of the grindier precons I’ve seen, and I&nbsp;
didn’t want to handicap its ability to win by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:17.440
cutting perfectly passable pressure options&nbsp;
just because they could be a bit better.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:21.360
I will also reiterate that money will go further&nbsp;
in upgrading this deck than it would for a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:25.920
precons. Because the card pool is so narrow,&nbsp;
there are great cards that use -1 counters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:29.920
that don’t really have budget replacements, and&nbsp;
you’d probably want to eventually snag those if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:34.240
you’re trying to upgrade this deck to a fully&nbsp;
functional bracket 3 form. I will also say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:39.360
that narrow card pool itself is part of why I like&nbsp;
this precon so much. It’d be easy for this to end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:43.680
up being a precon that feels a bit one-trick-pony&nbsp;
and really constrained in how you could go about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:48.880
improving it, but instead the opposite is true.&nbsp;
This deck has a reasonable alternative commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:53.680
multiple possible modification paths presenting&nbsp;
themselves, and it contains fewer obvious cuts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.680 --> 00:12:58.240
than any other precon I’ve reviewed. Part of that&nbsp;
comes down to the cards that are in the deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:03.440
but another big part comes down to the design of&nbsp;
the commander. Auntie Ool is a great enabler for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:07.680
-1 counters that encourages diving deep into&nbsp;
the mechanic and playing around with synergy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:11.600
and that’s cool to see. The overall package&nbsp;
works well, and if you’re trying to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:15.680
somebody into commander who is a fan of&nbsp;
other cardgames or roguelike deck builders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:20.560
this is going to be a deck with a sweet aesthetic&nbsp;
and a lot of fun synergies for them to chew on.

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:23.280
Thanks for watching, and thanks to&nbsp;
my supporters for helping me to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:27.360
what I do here. If you want to join them in&nbsp;
supporting me, you can click the join button,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:31.040
or follow the link to my patreon down in&nbsp;
the description. You’ll be getting access&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:34.480
to a back catalogue of bonus videos I’ve&nbsp;
made, and you may get the chance to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:38.480
your deck reviewed by me in one of my&nbsp;
quarterly deck reviews. Alright, bye.

